{"kind":"report","text":"Early I1 step 2 static review at c05e89cfd1d6332fce6f607df09087b3f1c50e77, under request 73d7b4ab2ed56494bfad01aa3ce55f6ca5cd15bf and promise 7f78a483341bd1bf13fdcd98bceeca5ae6d54df6.\n\nThis is preliminary feedback on committed supported forms, not an ordinary final I1 verdict, R1 adoption or runtime certification. The live complete I1 commission 2186c3c2cffa8fa6f647189a429742eebee3c71a and R1 follow-through 1e85696dfd5140fff8d0f918c87f19f01b84ca45 remain the governing scope. No schedule estimate limits that scope.\n\nEight material P2 findings:\n\n1. Validate both operands of equality and difference. packages/derive/src/validate.ts:281–285 combines the two operand calls with short-circuit OR. When a is a valid field, b is never validated. Add {equals:{a:{field:\"owner\"},b:null}} to the small fixture's opening guards: the definition is admitted, then a valid owner write reaches guards.ts:50–54 and evaluates \"field\" in null, throwing. A b slot operand can also bypass the rule against reading a nascent slot (adopted R1 v7:743–745). The current validator test for a nascent operand exercises only a. Evaluate both validators before combining their field flags; retain shape, type and nascent checks for each operand.\n\n2. Copying a field into a value slot ignores destination bounds. validate.ts:74–76 compares only type names; value-copy validation at 434–437 therefore accepts a larger field bound, while effects.ts:77–81 copies without destination validation. In the small fixture, change note.values.text.of.max to 4 while keeping the text field's max at 200; its constant \"late\" remains valid. A valid opening write with text \"abcde\" stores five bytes in a four-byte slot. Integer and list bounds have the same gap. Require assignment compatibility for the destination or validate the actual destination value before committing. Preserve distinct equality-comparison rules where narrower assignment rules are unnecessary.\n\n3. A ref copy admits source value slots but reads only ref slots. validate.ts:423–425 accepts a same-typed source slot of either kind; effects.ts:75 reads item.refs[from.slot] only. Add a value slot backup of type scope/lane with a valid scope reference as default, and a mutable optional ref peer; the effect {ref:{slot:\"peer\",from:{slot:\"backup\"}}} passes validation yet copies null rather than the reference. Adopted R1 v7:950 says another slot, without this source-kind restriction. Align the supported grammar and the reader so an admitted copy preserves its source; do not silently replace a present reference with null.\n\n4. Fact hash deduplication bypasses complete fact identity checking. judge.ts:124 skips later fact leaves when their hash is already in facts, before the at/seq/hash check at 128. Two byte-ordered fields a and z may contain a valid {at:L,seq:3,hash:H} and a well-shaped false {at:Other,seq:99,hash:H}. After a resolves L.3, z is skipped and its fact guard (guards.ts:139–144) reads L.3 by H. The intent can therefore claim an unverified scope and position while using a different fact; the uses list records only a. Verify every complete FactRef before deduplicating fetches or recorded uses. Keep exact scope and sequence binding even when hashes coincide. The existing foreign-fact test covers only one proof field.\n\n5. An admitted timed effect can throw and leave the due drain permanently blocked. The validator permits bounded party-list add effects (396–412), own-subject timed effects (472–481), and a transition out of the due state (648–651). judge.ts:218–220 nevertheless assumes timed effects cannot refuse and throws on refusal. A reachable live job in waiting can have mutable owner A, a mutable members list with max 1 containing B, and a future deadline. A timed rule with effects [{state:\"done\"},{party:{slot:\"members\",from:{slot:\"owner\"},list:\"add\"}}] passes validation. At the deadline A cannot fit, effects.ts:68–70 refuses slot-full, no entry commits, and the original job remains due. nextDue selects it again; required due draining then prevents ordinary progress (R1 v7:433–446, 491–497). Make admitted timed effects total, including capacity, or provide an explicit contract-supported disposition. Do not repair this by silently skipping the due item, advancing time without its entry, or claiming it settled.\n\n6. A final secondary subject can still be mutated. Adopted R1 v7:725–726 requires retained final items to refuse every transition. A live primary A and an also subject B already in a final state, with correct expected revisions, can accept a value effect on B. The validator requires live-state guards for state effects only (394); judgeAct:160–162 checks only the primary item's finality; effects.ts:49–52 checks finality only for state effects. The value effect changes B and fold.ts:119–122 increments its revision. Reject mutations to subjects that were final before the entry. Preserve read-only access to final subjects and valid atomic live-to-final entries whose later effects finish that same transition.\n\n7. The attribution cache loses contributors and principals in two supported histories. R1 v7:992–1004 requires all former holders and principals of attributed members who acted under other authority.\n(a) Extend the fixture report act with an existing also hold H, fields naming H and new holder V, and effects that change H's holder from signer U to V, renew H, then attribute from source commitment C. H is held and C accepted; the report can satisfy current guards and expected revisions. effects.ts:28–30 updates H, but attribution at 83–88 reads C's stale working cache. fold.ts:128–132 propagates H's new attribution to C only after entry effects were selected. The report omits V, allowing a holder to be treated as outside its authors.\n(b) Rita opens a small note with owner Una under no Una grant; Una later edits its mutable text using a current grant whose principal is Paul. The accepted value edit passes the existing owner/deadline guards, yet changeItem at fold.ts:71 never records Paul. A later attribution copy includes Una and omits Paul.\nMaintain attribution for the relevant accepted history, including same-entry hold changes and principals of actual later authority, or derive it from retained history. Existing tests cover separate-entry takeover followed by report, not these two cases. The delta note's cache proposal does not discharge these contract duties.\n\n8. MemoryState's bounded queries scan all retained history, making a growing legal history quadratic to replay. state.ts:101–103 copies every retained item, filters and sorts, then slices to limit. judgeAct:98 asks nextDue before every new act; timed.ts:26–28 uses a bounded live query, but MemoryState scans all ended items too. Repeatedly open and end holds while keeping at most two live: final holds remain retained, as required by R1 v7:727–736, so per-act work grows with history even with no due item. Across the sequence this scan alone is O(n²); range paging at state.ts:79–90 repeats the scan for each page. Implementation plan line 39 assigns type/state indexes, but only counts are indexed. Maintain ordered per-type/state buckets and correct cursor/more behavior through putItem. Do not introduce a retained-item quota or evict final items. This is a static complexity finding about the actual MemoryState used by tests and verification, not a measured 10× result or a claim about a future durable StateView.\n\nCoverage and evidence: checker read all 12 packages/derive/src files in full (112632 characters of numbered source), all three tests, the complete 319-line fixture, complete derive README, all 81 lines of notes/2026-10-04-i1-contract-deltas.md, current definition.ts, and the complete changed contract delta from 82f6c3b24ad0f2c3383dcc6e70f66011093ba797 through this head. The implementation plan's full prior read transfers unchanged; adopted R1 v7 is the fully read 05244cfbcd2619c877cbcfcac5985b47651bb31a base, with pertinent finality, nascent, ref and attribution clauses reread. Four parallel static audits independently covered fact/timed behavior, validation/starter forms, lifecycle/attribution, and bounded-read/test economy; checker independently read the production paths and tests before combining findings. Frozen source commits are c2aafd8291d5da8d7910ff772a13af39b55c90bb (derive) and f4be0ad78ee8e105b93fac57250284eb2dbe3ed5 (contract extensions). Exact delta SHA-256 is 495d621f0552c89a86392ac3cd95b0b1d7d0b14aee4771f5bdc6447f3dfcb9d9; validator ad531dfd3a4f504ff933bd58b7aff236946b38dc9afee31bff467ba81969e5e8; judge 658237935dbc0ab70b62406fb468c2ddf0117394549c99dcfa08ff801cbfc6d8; effects 64f3940d0bae5487b1726e23677ab6d507de2187eff9f192f3b8dbb20a946d7c; fold f2c423ae31d369ea21c640492feda4e19ae01b064e645105fa93412e29e66ec3; state 4613a0d7440b55bc94b59ce62c4bd269060814552bfb46acd522b9ff91928f23. These are static witnesses, not executed test results.\n\nOwned choices and remaining scope: all 43 proposed contract deltas remain unadopted pending actual R1 disposition. The source's expiry-before-idempotency order conflicts with the adopted retry rule, but builder progress 475bd022 already explicitly assigns the change to consult the index before notAfter; this report adds no duplicate finding and does not claim c05 already fixes it. The generic per-field byte/signature sweep is separately owned by request 520ebceb94063fc37fdd788f6ee5df3fc6661b71; no duplicate test-economy request is needed. The current replay helper folds recorded effects, without independent judgment/verifier/restart/storage/transport evidence. Grants.current and fixture profile tables remain test authority ports, not production admission. Unsupported genesis/delivery/diagnosis/outcome/checkpoint judges remain explicitly deferred. Production profile/auth admission, all supported forms, real Durable Object restart and Service Binding evidence, source parking/removal duties and the complete cross-package ordinary review remain owed under the live I1 commission. Builder-reported 103 tests/typecheck and roughly two seconds are producer attribution only. Checker ran no project code, tests, build, install, typecheck, private diagnostic or provider probe, and changed no source.\n\nApply these corrections as the existing I1 implementation proceeds, then bring the complete supported source and actual adopted contract dispositions to ordinary review. These findings add no parallel engine, quota, gate or reduction of functional scope."}